<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · island</p><h1 class="enc-title">Comoro Islands</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Comoros, Union of the Comoros</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Comoros are an archipelago in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indian-ocean">Indian Ocean</a> between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mozambique">Mozambique</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madagascar">Madagascar</a>, comprising <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/grande-comore">Grande Comore</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moheli">Mohéli</a>, Anjouan and Mayotte. Settled by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bantu-peoples">Bantu-speaking</a> and Austronesian peoples, they developed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/swahili">Swahili</a> and Islamic sultanates linked to East African trade. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> annexed them in the 19th century; three islands became the independent Union of the Comoros in 1975, while Mayotte remained French.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/comoro-islands.jpg" alt="Comoro Islands" width="320" height="379" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Comoros_rel91.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
